WEBVTT

00:00:00.027 --> 00:00:06.622
Three names appear often when we talk about subword tokenization: BPE, WordPiece, and SentencePiece.

00:00:06.622 --> 00:00:13.442
Byte-Pair Encoding, or BPE, repeatedly merges frequent patterns so common sequences become reusable tokens.

00:00:13.442 --> 00:00:18.647
WordPiece, associated historically with BERT-style tokenizers,

00:00:18.647 --> 00:00:25.717
also builds a vocabulary of useful subword pieces, although its vocabulary-learning procedure is different.

00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:34.727
SentencePiece is a toolkit that can learn subword vocabularies directly from raw text without assuming that

00:00:34.727 --> 00:00:35.972
spaces already define word boundaries.

00:00:35.972 --> 00:00:41.262
Modern model families may also use byte-level BPE or other variations.

00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:49.502
For applied work, you do not need to implement all of these algorithms from scratch.

00:00:49.502 --> 00:00:55.447
The practical rule is much simpler: load the tokenizer provided for the checkpoint,

00:00:55.447 --> 00:01:01.621
inspect how it segments your data, and verify that it handles your language and domain reasonably well.
